"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 7.8. The surgical findings included a Gleason 4+3 with tertiary pattern 5 (ISUP grade group 3), pT2a stage, positive surgical margins, and no seminal vesicle invasion or lymph node metastasis. The biopsy grade (Gleason 4+3, ISUP grade group 2) differed from the prostatectomy specimen grade. The absence of lymph node metastasis is a favorable finding.\n\nCountervailing factors include the positive surgical margins and the discrepancy between biopsy and resection pathology. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly widens the uncertainty regarding the subsequent trajectory. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and its subsequent trend is crucial for refining the prognosis. The current heuristic sensitivity range for recurrence is provided by the established horizon. The fixed estimated horizon is 46.70 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 35.03–58.38 months; its coverage has not been validated."
